    "markdown": "# Project Scheduling\n\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\nThis chapter is in preparation. It will be built from **Lecture 8 -- Project\nSchedule** of MGMT-3105 and the `SampleGantt better.xlsx` and `gantt-chart_L.xlsx`\nworksheets, following the same structure as [Chapter 1](01-Sustainability.qmd)\nand [Chapter 9](09-Cost-Estimation-and-Budgeting.qmd).\n:::\n\n## Learning objectives (planned)\n\nBy the end of this chapter you will be able to:\n\n- Convert a network and duration estimates into a Gantt chart and a schedule baseline.\n- Apply three-point (PERT) estimating: $t_e = (o + 4m + p)/6$ and activity variance.\n- Compute the probability of meeting a target date using the project standard deviation.\n- Compress a schedule with crashing and fast-tracking, and find the least-cost crash sequence.\n- Explain resource-limited scheduling and schedule risk.\n- Read and maintain a schedule: milestones, baselines, and slippage.\n\n## Topic outline\n\n1. From AON to a time-scaled schedule.\n2. Deterministic vs. probabilistic durations; PERT.\n3. Probability of on-time completion.\n4. Milestones and the schedule baseline.\n5. Schedule compression: crashing and fast-tracking, and their costs and risks.\n6. Gantt charts and tracking Gantts.\n7. Running case: the schedule for the RC-vehicle project.\n",
